## Specific Arabic Rules
1. Grammatical Markers & Affixes: Do not include English structural markers (like the possessive 's) inside the target [...] if they are not part of the literal translation of the Arabic word. Leave them attached but outside the brackets (e.g., [my aunt]'s).

## Example input 1:

كذبة وصدقتا => A lie and I believed it
كانت أكبر كذبة عشتا => It was the biggest lie I lived
إني انساك ومن وقتا => That I would forget you, and since then
ما قدرت نسيت => I couldn't forget
عم عاند ماضي => I'm resisting the past

## Expected output 1:

[كذبة] وصدقتا => [A lie] and I believed it
كذبة [وصدقتا] => A lie [and I believed it]

[كانت] أكبر كذبة عشتا => [It was] the biggest lie I lived
كانت [أكبر] كذبة عشتا => It was [the biggest] lie I lived
كانت أكبر [كذبة] عشتا => It was the biggest [lie] I lived
كانت أكبر كذبة [عشتا] => It was the biggest lie [I lived]

[إني] انساك ومن وقتا => [That] I would forget you, and since then
إني [انساك] ومن وقتا => That [I would forget you], and since then
إني انساك [ومن] وقتا => That I would forget you, [and since] then
إني انساك ومن [وقتا] => time # use custom word translation because وقتا is better translated to "time" when on its own

[ما قدرت] نسيت => [I couldn't] forget
ما قدرت [نسيت] => I couldn't forget

[عم عاند] ماضي => [I'm resisting] the past # translated عم عاند as a single phrase because it functions as a single text unit
عم عاند [ماضي] => I'm resisting [the past]


## Example input 2:

بيت خالتي => my aunt's house

## Expected output 2:

[بيت] خالتي => my aunt's [house]
بيت [خالتي] => [my aunt]'s house

## Example input 3:
نهار التلاتا الصبح => Tuesday morning

## Expexted output 3:
[نهار التلاتا] الصبح => [Tuesday] morning # "نهار التلاتا" literally means "day of Tuesday" but maps logically to "Tuesday"
نهار التلاتا [الصبح] => Tuesday [morning]
